-- title:
--   Embedding the ℚ̄-rational q-expansion field of X(Γ) into ℂ(X(M))
-- statement:
--   Let $M$ be a nonzero natural number, let $\Gamma\le \mathrm{SL}_2(\mathbb{Z})$ be a subgroup containing the principal congruence subgroup $\Gamma(M)$ and the translation matrix `ModularGroup.T`, let $\iota\colon \overline{\mathbb{Q}}\to\mathbb{C}$ be a ring homomorphism, and let $K$ be a field that is simultaneously a $\mathbb{C}$-algebra and an algebra over [`ModularCurve.LevelN.ring M`](def/ModularCurve_LevelNFunctionField.html#L28) — the $\mathbb{C}$-subalgebra of functions $\mathbb{H}\to\mathbb{C}$ generated by $j=E_4^3/\Delta$ together with the Fricke functions $f_v$ for the nonzero $v\in(\mathbb{Z}/M)^2$ — compatibly as a scalar tower, and which is a fraction field of that subalgebra. Write $A$ for the intermediate field [`ModularCurve.laurentBaseChange`](def/ModularCurve_LaurentCoeff.html#L103) of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}((q))$ over $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ generated by the coefficientwise images of [`ModularCurve.qExpFunctionFieldC ℚ Γ`](def/ModularCurve_X1.html#L101), the latter being the subfield of $\mathbb{Q}((q))$ generated over $\mathbb{Q}$ by the quotients $\mathrm{intSeriesC}\,p_f/\mathrm{intSeriesC}\,p_g$ attached to pairs of modular forms $f,g$ of a common weight for $\Gamma$ with integral $q$-expansions $p_f,p_g$ and $\mathrm{intSeriesC}\,p_g\ne 0$. The assertion is that there exist a ring homomorphism $\Phi\colon A\to K$ and a $\mathbb{C}$-algebra homomorphism $E\colon K\to\mathbb{C}((q))$ such that: $E$ sends the image in $K$ of the generator $j\in$ [`ModularCurve.LevelN.ring M`](def/ModularCurve_LevelNFunctionField.html#L28) to [`ModularCurve.qExpand ℂ M (ModularCurve.jqModC ℂ)`](def/ModularCurve_X0.html#L25), the $j$-series $q^{-1}E_4^3\eta^{-24}$ with $q$ replaced by $q^M$; for every $u\in A$, $E(\Phi u)$ is the series obtained from $u$ by transporting coefficients along $\iota$ and substituting $q^M$ for $q$; $\Phi$ restricted to $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ is $\iota$ followed by $\mathbb{C}\to K$; any $y\in A$ whose underlying Laurent series is [`ModularCurve.jqModC (AlgebraicClosure ℚ)`](def/ModularCurve_JqCoeff.html#L15) is sent by $\Phi$ to the image of $j$ in $K$; and for each $\gamma\in\Gamma$, given that $G\mapsto G(\gamma^{-1}\cdot{-})$ preserves [`ModularCurve.LevelN.ring M`](def/ModularCurve_LevelNFunctionField.html#L28), every $\mathbb{C}$-algebra automorphism $\varphi$ of $K$ inducing $G\mapsto G(\gamma^{-1}\cdot{-})$ on that subalgebra fixes $\Phi u$ for all $u\in A$.
--
--   This is the comparison, in Shimura's theory of canonical models, between the $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-rational $q$-expansion field of $X(\Gamma)$ and the function field of the level-$M$ modular curve, realised concretely through $q$-expansions: the embedding $\Phi$ is pinned down by $\iota$ on constants, by $j\mapsto j$, and by compatibility with the substitution $q\mapsto q^M$, and its image lands in the $\Gamma$-invariants of $K$. It is used in the level-$N$ base-change statement and in the computations of orders of vanishing on the $\Gamma_0$- and $\Gamma_1$-curves over $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$.
